{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1789333152,"nonce":"x7g/fVno/t7646TlsvZjA/kR3Hw=","title":"Python fib(40) is 40x slower than the same ZynML","message":"Three-way, 3 runs, medians (`bench_runner --filter fib --no-cache`):\n\n    fib(40)   zynml 11.3 ms   zypy 465 ms (compile 47 ms)   cpython 12933 ms\n\nThe other call kernels are within 1.3x of ZynML, so this is not call\noverhead in general. ZynML's `fib` collapses under pure-call PRE: the\nfunction is inferred pure and the two recursive calls share work across\nthe recursion. The Python `fib` carries a pending-exception check after\neach call (the callee may raise), which is a load of a global and a branch,\nand the global load makes the function impure to the purity inference, so\nPRE never sees it.\n\nOptions, in order of reach: mark functions that cannot raise (no fatal\npath reachable, every callee also cannot raise) and emit no check after\ncalls to them; let the purity inference treat a load of the pending global\nas pure when the function stores nothing to it; or both. Related to\n5caf6de (pending check overhead).","files":null}]}